<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    nexus-22r1f (P0, reproduced against the DEPLOYED cloud engine 0.1.56).

    nexus.memory.fts_vector tokenized titles as SINGLE opaque lexemes:
    PostgreSQL's text-search parser classifies 'auth-design.md' as a FILE
    token and keeps it whole, whereas SQLite FTS5's unicode61 tokenizer
    splits on every non-alphanumeric and emits 'auth', 'design', 'md'.
    plainto_tsquery('english','auth') therefore had nothing to match, and a
    title was findable in service mode ONLY by its exact full string, or when
    it contained no separator at all.

    Measured, same store method, same inputs (bead table):

        title                       query          sqlite  service
        RDR-025-implementation.md   RDR-025             1        0
        auth-design.md              auth                1        0
        auth-design.md              auth-design         1        0
        auth-design.md              auth-design.md      1        1
        plaintitle                  plaintitle          1        1

    '<name>.md' is the dominant T2 title convention, and SERVICE has been the
    storage default since 6.0 — so this is a silent wrong answer on a
    default-on read path: the search succeeds, returns 200, and reports zero
    hits. Confirmed on a real populated install, not a fresh test tenant:
    memory_search('nonrelocatable') returned nothing while memory_search
    ('dyld') returned the very entry whose TITLE contains 'nonrelocatable'.

    THIS IS THE SECOND FALSIFICATION OF THE SAME PARITY CLAIM. catalog-015
    fixed the identical bug on catalog_documents in 2026-07-13 (nexus-8gue1,
    GH #1397) and its comment already recorded that "the catalog-001 'PG >=
    FTS5 superset' claim was false for filenames". memory-001 makes the same
    superset claim (docs/rdr/rdr-152-fts-parity-contract.md Store 1) and it is
    false for exactly the same reason. This changeset is deliberately a
    line-for-line application of the catalog-015 shape so the two tables stay
    reasoned about together.

    Fix: a fourth OR'd segment that normalizes the separator characters
    / . - _ to spaces before tokenizing, so 'auth-design.md' also yields
    'auth','design','md'. Weight 'A' matches the title segment it augments —
    a title-fragment hit should rank like a title hit, not below content.
    Existing segments are untouched, so every previously-matching query still
    matches (superset preserved, never narrowed).

    SCOPE, deliberately narrow: TITLE only. content is prose and already
    tokenizes correctly; tags already use the 'simple' config. The measured
    defect is the title column, and widening the STORED expression beyond the
    evidence would grow the index for no demonstrated gain.

    PG cannot ALTER a generated column's expression: DROP + re-ADD (table
    rewrite) + recreate the GIN index. ACCESS EXCLUSIVE for the rewrite is
    fine in the single-instance rm-then-run deploy window (nothing serves
    during boot — same framing as catalog-014/015); Liquibase runs the
    changeset in one transaction, so an interrupted rewrite rolls back whole,
    never a dropped-column limbo. DDL only, no row DML, so the FORCE-RLS
    silent-no-op class (nexus-1wjmq) does not apply.

    DOES NOT FIX the other two divergences recorded on nexus-22r1f — accent
    folding (needs the unaccent extension on BOTH sides) and prefix-query
    syntax ('auth*', query-side only). Those are separate changes with
    separate risks; see the bead.
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <changeSet id="memory-002-0" author="nexus-22r1f">
        <comment>fts_vector: add separator-normalized title segment so dotted titles (auth-design.md) are findable by any word inside them (auth)</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true">
DROP INDEX IF EXISTS nexus.idx_memory_fts;
ALTER TABLE nexus.memory DROP COLUMN fts_vector;
ALTER TABLE nexus.memory
    ADD COLUMN fts_vector TSVECTOR GENERATED ALWAYS AS (
        setweight(to_tsvector('english', coalesce(title,   '')), 'A') ||
        setweight(to_tsvector('english', coalesce(content, '')), 'B') ||
        setweight(to_tsvector('simple',  coalesce(tags,    '')), 'C') ||
        setweight(to_tsvector('simple',
            translate(coalesce(title, ''), '/.-_', '    ')), 'A')
    ) STORED;
CREATE INDEX idx_memory_fts ON nexus.memory USING GIN (fts_vector);
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true">
DROP INDEX IF EXISTS nexus.idx_memory_fts;
ALTER TABLE nexus.memory DROP COLUMN fts_vector;
ALTER TABLE nexus.memory
    ADD COLUMN fts_vector TSVECTOR GENERATED ALWAYS AS (
        setweight(to_tsvector('english', coalesce(title,   '')), 'A') ||
        setweight(to_tsvector('english', coalesce(content, '')), 'B') ||
        setweight(to_tsvector('simple',  coalesce(tags,    '')), 'C')
    ) STORED;
CREATE INDEX idx_memory_fts ON nexus.memory USING GIN (fts_vector);
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
